// pf_eave.vfl - B5's INPUT.  PRIM wrangle: arm the SAME inset rule for the
// roof's overhang, and flatten the loops so one straight skeleton serves the
// whole stream.
//
// ⚠️ THE OVERHANG IS AN INSET WITH A NEGATIVE DISTANCE, so B5 reuses
// `pf_inset.vfl` unchanged - the third caller of the one rule, after B1's
// setback and B2's courtyard tract.  That is not thrift: an OUTWARD offset of
// a non-convex polygon is the mirror of the inward one, so the reflex corner
// that G2 exists to test is exercised in BOTH directions by one file, and the
// index correspondence `pf_inset` preserves is what lets the roof's eave edge
// be matched to the wall it sits on.
//
// ⚠️ FLATTENED TO y = 0 ON PURPOSE.  `polyexpand2d` fits ONE plane through its
// whole input (`planepossrc` = fitplane), so two buildings whose plinths level
// to different datums would be skeletonised on a TILTED plane - and with this
// fixture's flat ground they would not, which is exactly the shape of defect
// this project keeps finding ("a fixture property was load-bearing without
// saying so", round-1 defect 3).  `pf_cap.vfl` puts each roof back on its own
// building's datum afterwards, so nothing depends on the loops being level.
//
// Same single-owner argument for the point write as `pf_facade_in.vfl`.
float eave = f@_eave;
int pts[] = primpoints(0, @primnum);
int nv = primvertexcount(0, @primnum);

for (int j = 0; j < nv; j++)
    setvertexattrib(0, "_inset", @primnum, j, -eave);

foreach (int pt; pts) {
    vector p = point(0, "P", pt);
    setpointattrib(0, "P", pt, set(p.x, 0.0, p.z));
}
